\par }\pard \ql \li720\ri0\sb60\sa60\widctlpar\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof \{bmc security properties - anti-spam 256c.bmp\}
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f3\fs20\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \ql \fi-360\li720\ri0\sb60\sa60\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx720\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls148\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0 {\b\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof Block mail from unknown hosts}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof 
. Most legitimate sources of email are hosts that can be located in the DNS. In other words, these machines are well-established and correspond to known }{\b\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof host.domain}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof 
 names. Many systems used to distrubute spam are not defined in the DNS, and are accessible only through TCP/IP address (i.e., }{\b\f2\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof 12.34.56.78}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof ). Be aw
are that blocking mail from unknown hosts is a very conservative policy and may lead to legitimate mail being blocked from your users.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f3\fs20\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}{\b\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof Block mail from blacklisted hosts}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof . Sources of high-volume spam are tracked by }{
\cs100\lang1024\langfe1024\super\noproof K{\footnote \pard\plain \s99\ql \li360\ri0\sb60\sa60\widctlpar\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0 
\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs100\super K}{ Blacklist}}}{\i\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof blacklist services}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof , which
 provide lists of sites that have been responsible for such mailings. MailSite allows you to block all messages from hosts named by a blacklist service, and also allows you to select the blacklist service you wish to use. The default service is the MAPS R
ealtime Blackhole List (RBL). Use of this feature is recommended for ISP sites.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f3\fs20\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}{\b\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof Block mail from specific hosts}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof 
. In addition to unknown hosts and blacklisted hosts, you can block mail from any other source. You can choose to block mail from servers based on 
hostname or IP address. As with other blocking methods, this prevents the named system from sending any mail - spam or otherwise - to your site, so it should be used conservatively.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f3\fs20\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}{\b\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof Block mail from specific users}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof . Mail can also be blocked from specific use
rs, as defined by the envelope return address (}{\b\f2\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof MAIL FROM}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof 
). This option is particularly useful if one or more specific users are sending junk mail to your users. When blocking addresses, you can use wildcard notation (}{\b\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof *@domain.com}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof 
) to block mail coming from all addresses within a domain.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\f3\fs20\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}{\b\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof Set maximum recipients per message}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof 
. Because spam is typically sent to large numbers of users, setting a maximum on the number of recipients per message allows you to combat mass mailings. When you set a limit to the number o
f recipients, messages sent to more than this number of addresses (as given by the }{\b\f2\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof RCPT TO}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof 
 command) will have all recipients after the maximum number rejected. For example, if you set a maximum of 20, and a message arrives for 50 of your users, only the first 20 users will receive the message.
\par }\pard \ql \li360\ri0\sb60\sa60\widctlpar\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0 {\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof 
All of these policies can be set in the Anti-Spam tab of the Security Properties window. MailSite also provides an Anti-Spam wizard that leads you through the process of implementing an anti-spam strategy for your sit
e. Using the wizard is highly recommened, particularly for new users. For more information, double-click the }{\b\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof Anti-Spam Wizard}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof  icon in the MailSite Console's Server folder.
\par Experienced users can also use mask lists to specify hosts and addresses which should be blocked from sending mail to your site. These lists can be opened from the Anti-Spam Tab of the Security Properties window by clicking the buttons }{
\b\f2\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof Reject all incoming mail from these addresses}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof , and }{\b\f2\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof Reject all incoming mail from these hosts}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof . See the following section on 
}{\uldb Advanced SMTP Security}{\v\uldb AdvancedSMTPSecurity}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof  for more information.
\par }\pard\plain \s31\ql \li360\ri0\sb60\sa60\widctlpar\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\uldb Mail Filters}{\v\uldb 
MailFilters}{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof  (described in the following chapter) provide an additional mechanism to combat spam. Filters allow you to search the contents of the message
 (headers, body, and attachments) for specific  content and block it from entering your system. For example, using filters you can reject all mail that contains the subject "Make money fast!", regardless of the host used to send the mail.
\par }\pard\plain \s3\ql \li360\ri0\sb60\sa60\widctlpar\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0 \b\i\f1\fs24\cf10\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
