\i negation}{.  The list of names can be read from a text file, or you can specify the names directly in the scrolling list.
\par If you plan to maintain a list of names in a text file, select the }{\b\f2 Read mask list from this file}{ option and enter the full name of 
the text file in the corresponding field.  If you are running the Windows Console on the same machine as you are administering, you can use the }{\b\f2 Browse}{
 button to browse for the file.  The text file must contain a list of name (or address) masks, one per line.
\par Alternatively, you can use the }{\b\f2 Specify mask list directly}{ option.  You may then enter a mask in the edit field to the left of the }{\b\f2 Add}{ button, and click }{\b\f2 Add}{ to add it to the list.  The order of items in the list is significant
--use the }{\b\f2 Up}{ and }{\b\f2 Down}{ buttons to move them around.  The }{\b\f2 Remove}{ button removes a single entry from the list, while }{\b\f2 Remove All}{
 clears the list entirely.  There are also buttons to allow you to import and export name lists to and from text files.
