Enter a list of domain names, separated by commas.  The list is processed from left to right.  You may specify }{\b\f2 *}{ as part of a domain name, representing a wildcard. You can also have an exclamation mark }{\b\f2 !}{
 in front of a domain name, indicating that matching domains don't belong in the list.
\par Messages for foreign domains that match this list will be delivered according to the elapsed time schedule.  By default, this field contains a single asterisk, meaning 
that all foreign domains use the elapsed time schedule.  If you wish all foreign domains to use the time-of-day schedule instead (for instance, if you have a dialup connection to the Internet), you should enter the value }{\b\f2 !*}{ in this field.
\par For example, suppose your MailSite system is located at company HQ, and is hosting a single domain }{\b\f2 engineering.mycompany.com}{.  It is running on the HQ Intranet that contains other mail servers, hosting domains like }{\b\f2 sales.mycompany.com}{
. Your company's Internet connection is established only at certain times of the day.  There is a branch office in another country, not connected to the company Intranet, but which has its own Internet connection and has a mail server hosting the domain }
{\b\f2 italy.mycompany.com}{. In these circumstances, you might want to set the domain mask string to:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s53 \f3\fs20 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'de\tab}}\pard\plain \s53\ql \fi-720\li1080\ri0\sb60\sa60\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx1080\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls62\pnrnot0
\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnsp120\pnhang{\pntxtb \'de}}\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls62\adjustright\rin0\lin1080\itap0 \b\f2\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {!italy.mycompany.com,*mycompany.com,!*
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li360\ri0\sb60\sa60\widctlpar\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {This ensures that mail to }{\b\f2 
users@italy.mycompany.com }{would be scheduled for delivery according to the time-of-day schedule, whereas mail to }{\b\f2 users@anywhere.else.mycompany.com }{
would be scheduled according to the elapsed time schedule (since it is destined for servers on the local Intranet). All other foreign mail would be delivered according to the time-of-day schedule.
\par Note that this field has nothing to do with routing, which is always performed according to the DNS and the manual routing table. In the above example, you might wish to route mail to }{\b\f2 italy.mycompany.com }{
and mail to domains outside the company via your ISP's mail server, and route mail to the other domains within your company in a different way. You should use the Routing dialog to achieve this.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\ql \li360\ri0\sb60\sa60\widctlpar\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel3\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0 \b\f2\fs20\ul\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
