Select the Mailboxes folder within a domain to display a list of mailboxes:
\par }{\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof \{bmc windows console - mailboxes - 256c.bmp\}}{
\par Mailboxes are slots for delivering messages for a specific user on this server. You can create four types of MailSite mailboxes (depending on your license key): Registry mailboxes, NT mailboxes, Database mailboxes, and SQL mailboxes.
\par If your users will access other components of your NT server, such as the file system, then you will proba
bly want to use NT Mailboxes.  This will mean that they can use the same user name and password to read their mail that they use to log into the Windows network and connect to the file system.
\par If your users will only use the server to send and receive mail, then you may wish to create Registry Mailboxes for them.  This will limit their privileges to mail and will definitely prevent them from accessing any other part of the NT system.
\par If you have a database that contains a list of usernames and passwords, and you wish to integrate mail authentication with this database, then create Database Mailboxes.
\par If you want to use clustering to support mailboxes across multiple systems, then create SQL mailboxes.
\par You can select the type of mailbox display in the right-h
and panel using one of the four leftmost buttons in the toolbar, or by using the View menu.  Refresh the mailbox display using the Refresh command in the View menu, or by clicking the Refresh button (the one with the two arrows).
\par You can maintain the properties for a mailbox by double clicking on the mailbox icon.  This will open the }{\uldb Mailbox General Page}{\v\uldb MailboxGeneralPage}{.
\par }\pard\plain \s4\ql \li360\ri0\sb60\sa60\widctlpar\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel3\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0 \b\f2\fs20\ul\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
