\par }{Specify the name of the target MailSite server. If this is omitted, the local machine is assumed. Note that the machine name must be preceded by a double backslash.
\par }{\b\f2 -restore
\par }{Read the configuration from the file and write it to the Registry. If this argument is omitted, the configuration will be read from the Registry and written to the file.
\par }{\b\f2 -deleteold
\par }{Delete the old configuration in the Registry before restoring the configuration from the file. If this argument is omitted, the configuration from the file will be merged with the existing configuration. This argument is only relevant if }{\b\f2 
-restore}{ is specified. 
\par }{\b\f2 filename
\par }{The name of the file to which the configuration will be written (or from which it will be read, if -restore was specified). This argument is required.
\par }{\b\f2 MSBACK}{ uses a text format to store the mail server configuration information. The format is described in }{\b\f2 BNF}{ below.  Examining a generated backup file will help make this clearer.
\par }\pard\plain \s53\ql \li360\ri0\sb60\widctlpar\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw20\brsp20 \brdrl\brdrs\brdrw20\brsp80 \brdrb\brdrs\brdrw20\brsp20 \brdrr\brdrs\brdrw20\brsp80 
