Specifies the name of a target remote machine (preceded by a double backslash), as well as login data for a mailbox on t
he target server that has MailSite server privileges. This option is used in conjunction with any of the other flags to execute the given operation on the remote system. If this is omitted, the local machine is assumed.
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li360\ri0\sb60\sa60\widctlpar\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0 \fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\b\f2 -list [}{\b\i\f2 domain}{
\b\f2 ]
\par }{List the mailboxes in the given domain. If the domain is omitted, the default domain is assumed.
\par }{\b\f2 -list2 [}{\b\i\f2 domain}{\b\f2 ] -security }{\b\i\f2 pass}{\b\f2 
\par }{List the mailboxes in the given domain. If the domain is omitted, the mailboxes in the default domain are listed.  The plugin ID, mailbox name, full 
name and password separated by tabs are listed.  Because this command reveals mailbox passwords, a security pass must be supplied.  The }{\b\i\f2 pass}{
 must be the password for the postmaster mailbox in the default domain.  Note that the postmaster mailbox must exist and must have a password that does not contain spaces.
\par }{\b\f2 -create }{\b\i\f2 name}{\b\f2 [@}{\b\i\f2 domain}{\b\f2 ] }{\b\i\f2 pluginid}{\b\f2 
\par }{Create a new mailbox called }{\b\f2 name}{ in virtual domain }{\b\f2 domain}{, using the mailbox identified by the }{\b\f2 pluginid }{(see below). If the domain is omitted, the mailbox is created in the default domain.
\par }{\b\f2 -delete }{\b\i\f2 name}{\b\f2 [@}{\b\i\f2 domain}{\b\f2 ]
\par }{Delete the mailbox }{\b\f2 name}{ in virtual domain }{\b\f2 domain}{. If the domain is omitted, the mailbox is deleted from the default domain.
\par }{\b\f2 -set }{\b\i\f2 name}{\b\f2 [@}{\b\i\f2 domain}{\b\f2 ] }{\b\i\f2 property}{\b\f2  }{\b\i\f2 value}{\b\f2 
\par }{Set the mailbox property }{\b\f2 property}{ to }{\b\f2 value}{.
\par }{\b\f2 -get name[@domain] property
\par }{Display the value of the named mailbox property.  See below for a list of property names.
\par }{\b\f2 -pass }{\b\i\f2 name}{\b\f2 [@}{\b\i\f2 domain}{\b\f2 ] [}{\b\i\f2 plaintextpassword}{\b\f2 ]
\par }{Set the mailbox password to }{\b\f2 plaintextpassword}{.
\par }{\b\f2 -createalias }{\b\i\f2 name}{\b\f2 [@}{\b\i\f2 domain}{\b\f2 ] }{\b\i\f2 alias}{\b\f2 [@}{\b\i\f2 domain}{\b\f2 ]
\par }{Create the given alias for the mailbox.  This operation is supported only for SQL mailboxes.
\par }{\b\f2 -deletealias }{\b\i\f2 alias}{\b\f2 [@}{\b\i\f2 domain}{\b\f2 ]
\par }{Deletes the given mailbox alias.  This operation is supported only for SQL mailboxes.
\par }{\b\f2 -listalias }{\b\i\f2 name}{\b\f2 [@}{\b\i\f2 domain}{\b\f2 ]
\par }{Lists the aliases for the given mailbox.  This operation is supported only for SQL mailboxes.
\par }\pard \ql \li360\ri0\sb60\sa60\widctlpar\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin360\itap0 {\b\f2 -plugin name[@domain] pluginid \line -plugin @filename pluginid
\par }{Change the plugin which is responsible for the mailbox.  The second form allows you to specify the name of a file containing a list of mailbox names, one per line.  The }{\b\f2 pluginid}{ indicates which plugin the mailbox should be moved to.
\par You can use this option to move mailboxes between Registry, NT User and Database plugins.  However, note that you cannot move a mailbox (called }{\b\f2 fred}{, say) to use the NT Us
er plugin or the Database plugin unless there is already an NT user with that name, or a database row with that value in the Mailbox column.
